ITEMS = ((('what are the signs of a heart attack', 'how do i recognise a heart attack', 'what does a heart attack feel like', 'which symptoms suggest a heart attack', 'how can i tell if it is a heart attack', 'what warning signs of a heart attack exist', 'how to spot a possible heart attack', 'what should i look for in a heart attack', 'how do i know if chest pain is a heart attack', 'what early signs of a heart attack are'), 'A heart attack may cause chest pain or pressure, pain in the arm, neck or jaw, breathlessness, sweating, nausea or a feeling of doom. Symptoms vary, especially in women and older adults. Call emergency services if you suspect one.'), (('what should i do if i think someone is having a heart attack', 'how to help a person having a heart attack', 'what is the first aid for a heart attack', 'how do you respond to a heart attack', 'what to do immediately for chest pain when recognising a heart attack', 'how can i assist during a heart attack', 'what helps someone with a heart attack', 'steps to take for a suspected heart attack', 'how should i care for a person with chest pain when recognising a heart attack', 'what is the best response to a heart attack'), 'Call emergency services as soon as a heart attack is suspected. Help the person rest in a comfortable position, keep them calm and loosen tight clothing. Stay with them and be ready to start CPR if they become unresponsive.'), (('should i call an ambulance for chest pain when recognising a heart attack', 'when to call for help with chest pain when recognising a heart attack', 'is chest pain always a heart attack for heart attack symptoms', 'what if chest pain comes and goes when recognising a heart attack', 'how urgent is chest discomfort when recognising a heart attack', 'should i drive someone with chest pain to hospital when recognising a heart attack', 'what if the pain is mild when recognising a heart attack', 'when to seek advice about chest pain when recognising a heart attack', 'what to tell the operator about chest pain when recognising a heart attack', 'who can assess chest pain when recognising a heart attack'), 'Call an ambulance for chest pain that is severe, persistent, or comes with breathlessness or sweating. Do not drive someone who may be having a heart attack. If pain is new or worrying, call anyway and let the operator advise.'), (('should i give aspirin for a heart attack', 'what medicine helps in a heart attack', "can i use someone's tablets for chest pain when recognising a heart attack", 'should i give medicine for suspected heart attack', 'what if the person has prescribed tablets when recognising a heart attack', 'is it safe to give tablets for chest pain when recognising a heart attack', 'when to ask a pharmacist about heart attack medicine', 'what not to give during a heart attack', 'how to handle medication in a heart attack', 'who can advise about heart attack drugs'), "Do not give any medicine unless it has been prescribed to that person and you have been told how to use it. Call emergency services first and follow the operator's advice. A qualified crew can give appropriate treatment at the scene."), (('what position is best for a heart attack', 'should i sit or lie someone with chest pain when recognising a heart attack', 'how to make a person comfortable when recognising a heart attack', 'should i let a person walk during a heart attack', 'what if the person wants to lie flat when recognising a heart attack', 'how to support someone with breathlessness when recognising a heart attack', 'should i loosen clothing for chest pain when recognising a heart attack', 'what if the person collapses when recognising a heart attack', 'how to keep a person calm with chest pain when recognising a heart attack', 'who can advise about positioning when recognising a heart attack'), 'Let the person rest in the position that helps them breathe, often sitting upright or half lying. Loosen tight clothing and reassure them. Call emergency services and stay nearby, ready to begin CPR if they stop responding.'), (('can a heart attack happen without chest pain', 'what are silent heart attack symptoms', 'can indigestion feel like a heart attack', 'what if pain is in the arm only when recognising a heart attack', 'how to recognise atypical heart attack signs', 'what if symptoms are subtle when recognising a heart attack', 'can a heart attack cause jaw pain', 'what if a woman has different symptoms when recognising a heart attack', 'when to suspect a heart attack in older adults', 'who can check unusual symptoms when recognising a heart attack'), 'A heart attack can occur with little or no chest pain, especially in women, older adults and people with diabetes. Breathlessness, nausea, sweating or pain in the jaw or arm may be the main signs. Call emergency services if you are concerned.'), (('what if someone collapses from a heart attack', 'should i start cpr for a heart attack', 'how to respond when a person stops responding when recognising a heart attack', 'what to do if breathing stops with chest pain when recognising a heart attack', 'should i fetch a defibrillator when recognising a heart attack', 'how to check breathing after collapse when recognising a heart attack', 'what if there is no pulse when recognising a heart attack', 'how long to continue cpr when recognising a heart attack', 'what to tell the ambulance crew when recognising a heart attack', 'who takes over after collapse when recognising a heart attack'), 'If someone collapses and is not breathing normally, call emergency services, start CPR and send someone for a defibrillator. Continue until a qualified team arrives. Tell them how long the person was unwell before collapsing.'), (('how can i lower the risk of a heart attack', 'what lifestyle changes help the heart', 'should i stop smoking for heart health', 'how does exercise affect heart risk', 'what foods support heart health', 'should i check my blood pressure when recognising a heart attack', 'how to manage stress for the heart', 'when to see a doctor about heart risk', 'what if heart disease runs in my family', 'who can advise on prevention when recognising a heart attack'), 'Not smoking, regular activity, a balanced diet and controlling blood pressure all lower heart attack risk. Ask a doctor or nurse about checks if you have risk factors such as family history. Seek urgent help for symptoms rather than waiting.'), (('what is the difference between angina and a heart attack', 'how to tell heart attack from indigestion', 'can panic feel like a heart attack', 'what if chest pain goes away when recognising a heart attack', 'how to judge chest symptoms when recognising a heart attack', 'what does a heart attack feel like compared with angina', 'when to treat chest pain as serious when recognising a heart attack', 'what if i am unsure about symptoms when recognising a heart attack', 'how to describe chest pain to a doctor when recognising a heart attack', 'who can investigate chest pain when recognising a heart attack'), 'Angina pain often settles with rest, while heart attack pain tends to persist and may come with sweating or breathlessness. It is not possible to be sure without tests. Call emergency services for new or worrying chest symptoms.'), (('what happens in hospital after a heart attack', 'how is a heart attack treated', 'what tests follow a heart attack', 'how long is recovery from a heart attack', 'what to expect after being discharged when recognising a heart attack', 'can i drive after a heart attack', 'what medicine might be prescribed when recognising a heart attack', 'how to recover at home after a heart attack', 'when to seek help again after a heart attack', 'who supports recovery from a heart attack'), 'Hospital care may include tests, medicines and procedures to restore blood flow, followed by a recovery plan. Ask the medical team about driving, activity and follow-up appointments. Call emergency services if symptoms return.'))
